Reproducibility and transparency
Live taxonomic databases change. Analyses using retrieved lineages should therefore record:

the taxodist version and implementation;
the retrieval date;
numeric Taxonomicon IDs, particularly for homonyms;
unresolved or excluded taxa;
whether live data or the packaged taxobase reference object was used;
the distance definition and downstream analysis settings.

The fixed taxobase object is distributed for examples, tests, and comparisons that must not depend on current server availability. It should not be assumed to represent the latest live classification.

Recommended reporting
A reproducible analysis should state:

the taxodist version and programming language;
the lineage source and retrieval date;
resolved Taxonomicon IDs and treatment of homonyms;
taxa excluded because of missing or disconnected lineages;
the distance definition and classification-depth interpretation;
clustering or ordination settings;
the permutation scheme and number of permutations;
checks for multivariate dispersion where PERMANOVA is used;
whether analyses used live lineages or the fixed taxobase object.

The R and Python packages are separate implementations of the same taxonomic hierarchy distance. Version 0.6.0 aligns their core behavioral contract:

ordered lineages are compared through their continuous common prefix;
identical complete lineages receive distance zero;
distinct nodes receive \(1 / \operatorname{depth}(\operatorname{MRCA})\);
lineages without a common root receive infinite distance;
unresolved lineages produce missing values in multi-taxon results;
ancestor–descendant pairs retain positive distance;
clade membership uses exact, case-insensitive matching;
the packaged reference data preserve the same component structure, taxa, order, values, and provenance.

Compatibility means equivalent scientific semantics and labeled results. It does not mean that every object has the same internal representation or printed appearance.

