Live retrieval may differ over time
The packages query an external website. Two runs can differ if they occur at different times, resolve different homonyms, encounter different network responses, or use different package versions.
For reproducible comparisons:

use the same taxodist version;
query the same numeric Taxonomicon IDs;
record the retrieval date;
verify lineage equality before comparing distances;
save language-specific caches when live records must be retained;
use taxobase for fixed examples and regression tests.

What compatibility does not guarantee
The project does not guarantee:

byte-identical serialized files;
identical console messages;
identical plot appearance;
identical internal clustering arrays;
identical eigenvector signs;
identical live results obtained from different source revisions.

It does guarantee a shared versioned definition and aims to preserve equivalent labels, distances, MRCA logic, edge-case handling, and reference data.

Dependence on the source classification
The distance depends on the number and arrangement of nodes recorded before the MRCA. It is therefore sensitive to:

taxonomic revision;
alternative classification systems;
insertion or removal of intermediate clades;
unequal resolution among taxonomic groups;
lineage-cleaning decisions;
ambiguous name resolution.

Adding an intermediate node to the shared prefix increases MRCA depth and decreases the numerical distance. Adding nodes only below the MRCA does not affect that pair.
Distances should consequently be compared only when derived from compatible lineage sources and processing rules.

Interpretation
A smaller finite value indicates a deeper shared node in the selected classification. It does not quantify:

elapsed evolutionary time;
nucleotide or amino-acid substitution;
morphological divergence;
phylogenetic branch length;
probability or support for common ancestry;
ecological or functional similarity.

A clustering dendrogram constructed from these values summarizes the classification-depth matrix. Conversion to class phylo changes the data representation, not the evidential basis of the tree.

Reproducibility
Live lineages may change when The Taxonomicon is updated. For empirical work, record:

taxodist version and implementation;
retrieval date;
queried names and resolved numeric IDs;
treatment of homonyms and redirects;
missing or excluded taxa;
lineage source and cleaning settings;
the distance definition;
downstream clustering, ordination, or permutation settings.

Use save_cache() when the exact retrieved lineages must be reused. Cache files are language-specific. Use taxobase when a fixed, packaged example is preferable to a live query.
